# Patch files must never be line-ending converted.
#
# `git apply` matches a patch's context lines byte-for-byte against the target
# file. The Chromium checkout is LF, so if core.autocrlf rewrites a .patch to
# CRLF on checkout — the Windows default, and the setting inherited by this
# project's self-hosted CI runner — every context line gains a trailing \r and
# the patch silently stops applying. That made patch success depend on each
# machine's local git config, which is exactly the kind of thing that works for
# whoever authored it and breaks in CI.
#
# -text disables all EOL conversion in both directions, keeping patches
# byte-identical everywhere.
*.patch -text

# Our overlay sources sit inside the Chromium tree and should match it (LF),
# regardless of what the checking-out machine's autocrlf says.
*.cc   text eol=lf
*.h    text eol=lf
*.gn   text eol=lf
*.gni  text eol=lf

# Repo-level files. PowerShell keeps CRLF, the Windows convention.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
